  "date": "07/09/2026",
  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Indexes Exist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scan Or Seek",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No index = COLLSCAN: every document read for one query.",
            "Index = ordered keys plus a `RecordId` pointer to the doc.",
            "MongoDB indexes are WiredTiger row-store B-trees, O(log n).",
            "`_id` index is automatic, unique, and cannot be dropped."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.users.createIndex({ age: 1 })   // 1 = ascending",
            "db.users.createIndex({ age: -1 })  // -1 = descending",
            "// auto name: age_1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How A Seek Works"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "root",
              "branch",
              "first key",
              "walk siblings",
              "RecordId",
              "FETCH"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Descend the tree, land on the first matching key, walk in order.",
            "Sorted keys serve equality, range and sort from one index.",
            "`$gt`, `$lt`, `$gte`, `$lte` are contiguous slices of the tree.",
            "Indexes are per-collection; one index per predicate branch."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Read The Explain"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.coll.find({email:'a@b.com'})",
            "  .explain('executionStats')",
            "// compare totalDocsExamined vs nReturned",
            "// healthy index query sits near 1:1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Stage",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "COLLSCAN",
              "whole collection read"
            ],
            [
              "IXSCAN",
              "index seek"
            ],
            [
              "FETCH",
              "load full document"
            ],
            [
              "PROJECTION_COVERED",
              "doc never touched"
            ],
            [
              "SORT",
              "blocking in-memory sort"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Price Of Every Index"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Costs disk and RAM in the WiredTiger cache.",
            "Slows every insert, update, delete touching indexed fields.",
            "10 indexes = 11 B-tree writes for one insert.",
            "Write amplification grows with each index you add."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Sort Memory Cap",
          "text": "In-memory sorts are capped at 100 MB and abort with `Sort exceeded memory limit`. An index in the sort order removes the SORT stage entirely."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Building Live",
          "items": [
            "Hybrid build yields to readers and writers.",
            "Still saturates disk I/O — schedule it.",
            "Start with `db.coll.createIndex({field: 1})`."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Single Field Indexes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Doc, One Key",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.users.createIndex({ age: 1 })",
            "// every document contributes exactly one key",
            "db.users.createIndex({ 'address.city': 1 })"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Direction is irrelevant here: the tree walks backwards fine.",
            "`{age:1}` serves `sort({age:1})` and `sort({age:-1})`.",
            "Serves equality `{age:30}` and range `{age:{$gte:18,$lt:65}}`.",
            "`$in` becomes a bounded multi-interval index scan."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bounds Tell The Truth"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "explain bounds",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`[\"[30, 30]\"]`",
              "equality seek"
            ],
            [
              "`[\"[18, 65)\"]`",
              "tight range, good"
            ],
            [
              "`[MinKey, MaxKey]`",
              "no selectivity at all"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Null And Missing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A document missing the field still gets a `null` key.",
            "`{age: null}` matches missing and explicitly-null docs alike.",
            "`createIndex({address:1})` indexes the whole subdocument.",
            "Whole-object equality only, and inner field order matters."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Selectivity Wins",
          "text": "An index on a boolean `isActive` split 50/50 rarely beats a collection scan. Index the fields that cut the candidate set hard."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Unique And Its Trap"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.users.createIndex({email:1},{unique:true})",
            "// duplicate: E11000 duplicate key error (11000)",
            "// two docs missing email -> both null -> 2nd fails"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sparse vs Partial"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "sparse",
                "lines": [
                  "Skips docs lacking the field",
                  "Smaller index",
                  "Cannot answer `{f: null}`",
                  "Cannot serve a full sort"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "partial",
                "lines": [
                  "Arbitrary predicate filter",
                  "Query must be a provable subset",
                  "Modern replacement",
                  "Pairs with `unique`"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Compound Indexes, Prefix Rule & ESR",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.users.createIndex({email:1},{",
            "  unique:true,",
            "  partialFilterExpression:{email:{$exists:true}}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Housekeeping",
          "items": [
            "`{hidden:true}`: maintained but invisible to the planner.",
            "Test a drop safely, unhide instantly if latency regresses.",
            "Auto name `age_1`; pass `{name:'idx_age'}` under 127 bytes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Ordered Tuple",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.orders.createIndex({",
            "  status: 1, createdAt: -1, userId: 1 })",
            "// hard ceiling: 32 fields per compound index"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "status",
              "createdAt",
              "userId",
              "RecordId"
            ],
            "indices": true,
            "pointers": [],
            "highlight": [],
            "dim": [],
            "caption": "one index key"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Keys sort lexicographically by the tuple, left to right.",
            "All `status` values group; inside each, `createdAt` descending."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Prefix Rule"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Query",
            "On `{a,b,c}`"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`{a}`",
              "seek"
            ],
            [
              "`{a,b}`",
              "seek"
            ],
            [
              "`{a,b,c}`",
              "seek"
            ],
            [
              "`{b}`",
              "no seek"
            ],
            [
              "`{b,c}`",
              "no seek"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only the leading contiguous subset can be seeked.",
            "No leading `a` means no start point in the tree.",
            "`{a,b,c}` makes `{a}` and `{a,b}` redundant — drop them."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "ESR Field Order"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Equality first, then Sort fields, then Range fields.",
            "Equality pins one contiguous slice of the tree.",
            "A range opens the slice; fields after it lose sorted order."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "find({status:'active', age:{$gt:21}})",
            "  .sort({createdAt:1})",
            "// want {status:1, createdAt:1, age:1}",
            "// not  {status:1, age:1, createdAt:1}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Wrong Order Still Runs",
          "text": "The index keeps filtering, but explain grows a `stage: 'SORT'` — a blocking sort bolted onto the scan. Check for it before shipping."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Covered Queries"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every field read and returned lives inside the index.",
            "Needs `_id: 0` in the projection unless `_id` is indexed.",
            "explain shows `totalDocsExamined: 0` — the strongest win."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sort Direction & Index-Backed Sorts",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two More Truths",
          "items": [
            "Non-prefix fields filter after the seek: IXSCAN, loose bounds.",
            "Index intersection exists but rarely beats one compound index.",
            "Design for query shapes: list your top queries first."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When Direction Matters",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Direction matters only in compound indexes, relative to siblings.",
            "`{a:1,b:-1}` serves `sort({a:1,b:-1})` — forward walk.",
            "The same index serves `sort({a:-1,b:1})` — backward walk.",
            "It does NOT serve `sort({a:1,b:1})`: one walk cannot flip one field."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Inverse Test",
          "text": "A sort is index-supported when the requested pattern equals the index pattern or its exact inverse — every field flipped, none left alone."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Prefix And Absorption"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sort keys must form an index prefix, or follow equality fields.",
            "`{status:1,createdAt:1}` plus `find({status:'A'})` sorts free.",
            "Equality pins `status` constant, so `createdAt` acts as prefix.",
            "`find({status:{$gt:'A'}})` absorbs nothing — blocking sort returns."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "index sort",
                "lines": [
                  "Streams the first doc instantly",
                  "`limit(10)` stops at 10 keys",
                  "No memory ceiling"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "blocking SORT",
                "lines": [
                  "Buffers every result first",
                  "`limit(10)` gains nothing",
                  "100 MB cap, then error"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pagination Payoff"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "find({status:'A'}).sort({createdAt:1}).limit(10)",
            "// stops after 10 index entries",
            "// skip(10000) still walks 10000 entries first"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Prefer range pagination on the sort key over `skip`.",
            "`{createdAt: {$lt: lastSeen}}` reseeks instead of re-walking.",
            "`allowDiskUse: true` spills a big sort — a bandage, not a fix."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Collation Note",
          "items": [
            "An index built with one collation cannot serve another.",
            "Mismatch kills both the sort and the equality match."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sort Edge Cases & Range Pagination",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Edge Cases"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Case",
            "Behaviour"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "multikey sort asc",
              "ordered by array min"
            ],
            [
              "multikey sort desc",
              "ordered by array max"
            ],
            [
              "`$natural`",
              "storage order, no index"
            ],
            [
              "collation mismatch",
              "index unusable"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Never Trust `$natural`",
          "text": "`$natural` ignores indexes and returns storage order. It is not an ordering guarantee — never build semantics on top of it."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Is The Sort Free?"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "q1"
              ],
              [
                "q2",
                "yes1"
              ],
              [
                "q3",
                "yes2"
              ],
              [
                "no"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "q1": {
                "text": "sort keys = index prefix?",
                "shape": "diamond"
              },
              "yes1": {
                "text": "IXSCAN order",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              "q2": {
                "text": "leading fields equality?",
                "shape": "diamond"
              },
              "yes2": {
                "text": "IXSCAN order",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              "q3": {
                "text": "pattern or full inverse?",
                "shape": "diamond"
              },
              "no": {
                "text": "blocking SORT",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            },
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "q1",
                "to": "q2",
                "label": "no"
              },
              {
                "from": "q1",
                "to": "yes1",
                "label": "yes"
              },
              {
                "from": "q2",
                "to": "yes2",
                "label": "yes"
              },
              {
                "from": "q2",
                "to": "q3",
                "label": "no"
              },
              {
                "from": "q3",
                "to": "no",
                "label": "no"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Sort on `{a:1,b:-1}`",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`{a:1,b:-1}`",
              "{green:forward walk}"
            ],
            [
              "`{a:-1,b:1}`",
              "{green:backward walk}"
            ],
            [
              "`{a:1,b:1}`",
              "{red:blocking SORT}"
            ],
            [
              "`{b:-1}` alone",
              "{red:not a prefix}"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Range Pagination"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// page 1",
            "find({status:'A'})",
            "  .sort({createdAt:-1}).limit(20)",
            "// next page: reseek, no skip",
            "find({status:'A',",
            "  createdAt:{$lt:lastSeen}})"
          ],
          "note": "`skip` still walks every entry"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Index `{status:1, createdAt:-1}` serves both pages.",
            "Pass the last `createdAt` seen as the cursor.",
            "Add `_id` as tiebreaker when timestamps collide.",
            "Cost stays flat on page 1 and page 500."
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Sort Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Range on the leading field, then sort on the next one.",
            "Mixed directions that are neither pattern nor inverse.",
            "`skip(10000)` for deep pages — walks 10000 keys.",
            "Query collation differing from the index collation."
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "`allowDiskUse` Is A Bandage",
          "text": "It spills a big sort to disk instead of erroring at 100 MB. The query stays slow — fix the index order instead."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Multikey Indexes (Arrays)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Arrays Index Themselves",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Index an array field and MongoDB makes it multikey automatically.",
            "No option, no flag, no separate command to run.",
            "One document produces N keys, one per array element.",
            "All N keys point back to the same `RecordId`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.posts.createIndex({ tags: 1 })",
            "// {tags:['db','mongo','index']} -> 3 keys",
            "db.orders.createIndex({ 'items.sku': 1 })"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "db | r7",
              "index | r7",
              "mongo | r7",
              "(sorted)"
            ],
            "indices": true,
            "pointers": [],
            "highlight": [],
            "dim": [],
            "caption": "keys for doc r7"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`find({tags:'mongo'})` seeks one key — membership is equality.",
            "Index size tracks total array cardinality, not document count.",
            "1M docs with 50-element arrays hold 50M index keys."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The One-Array Limit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A compound index may hold at most ONE array-valued field.",
            "Two arrays would demand a cartesian product of keys.",
            "An insert making both arrays fails: `cannot index parallel arrays`.",
            "The multikey flag is per-index and sticky — it survives the delete."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "No Covered Queries",
          "text": "The multikey flag disables index-only plans. Expect a FETCH on every multikey hit, however tight the bounds look in explain."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "`$elemMatch` Or Silent Bug"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "find({items:{$elemMatch:{sku:'A',qty:{$gt:5}}}})",
            "// SAME element must satisfy both",
            "find({'items.sku':'A','items.qty':{$gt:5}})",
            "// DIFFERENT elements may satisfy each"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The index narrows one condition; the other rechecks in FETCH.",
            "So multikey bounds are looser than explain makes them look."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Subdocument Arrays",
          "items": [
            "`createIndex({'items.sku':1})` indexes each element's `sku`.",
            "One key per element, same RecordId, same limits apply."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Parallel Arrays Fix"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "createIndex({tags:1, sizes:1})",
            "insert({tags:['a','b'],",
            "        sizes:[1,2]})",
            "// cannot index parallel arrays"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Multikey Sharp Edges & Explain Flags",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sharp Edges"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "On multikey"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "covered query",
              "impossible"
            ],
            [
              "sort + range on the array",
              "not index-served"
            ],
            [
              "`unique`",
              "no 2 docs share an element"
            ],
            [
              "TTL, hashed, text",
              "own array rules"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Spot It In Explain"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.posts.find({tags:'mongo'})",
            "  .explain('executionStats')",
            "// isMultiKey: true",
            "// multiKeyPaths: {tags:['tags']}"
          ],
          "note": "flag stays after arrays vanish"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`isMultiKey: true` means ==no covered plan== is possible.",
            "`multiKeyPaths` names which field made it multikey.",
            "Keys examined can exceed docs — one doc, many keys."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Index only the more selective array field.",
            "Keep the other field scalar, or move it to a child doc.",
            "Combine an array with scalars freely: `{tags:1, date:-1}`."
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Text Indexes: Queries, Language & Weights",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Tokenize And Stem",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "raw text",
              "tokenize",
              "drop stop words",
              "stem",
              "term keys"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.docs.createIndex({ description: 'text' })",
            "db.docs.createIndex({ title:'text', body:'text' })",
            "// only ONE text index per collection"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An inverted-style index over tokenized, stemmed, filtered terms.",
            "One text index per collection, but it may cover many fields.",
            "`createIndex({'$**':'text'})` indexes every string field.",
            "Wildcard text is cheap to type, expensive to run in production."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Writing A `$text` Query"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "find({$text:{$search:'mongodb index'}})   // OR-ed",
            "find({$text:{$search:'\\\"compound index\\\"'}}) // phrase",
            "find({$text:{$search:'mongodb -atlas'}})  // negated"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Terms are OR-ed by default; escaped quotes make a phrase.",
            "A leading minus negates a term inside the same search string.",
            "Score only via `{score:{$meta:'textScore'}}` in the projection.",
            "Sort it with `sort({score:{$meta:'textScore'}})`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Language And Weights"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Option",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`default_language`",
              "stemming + stop words"
            ],
            [
              "`language` field",
              "per-document override"
            ],
            [
              "`language:'none'`",
              "raw tokens, no stemming"
            ],
            [
              "`weights:{title:10}`",
              "title hit worth 10x"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use `'none'` for codes, identifiers, non-linguistic tokens.",
            "A text index is implicitly multikey — many term keys per doc.",
            "It is often larger on disk than the data it indexes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.docs.createIndex({ tenantId: 1, body: 'text' })",
            "// query MUST pass tenantId as equality"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "`$text` Limits & TTL Indexes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Monitor Loop"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "TTLMonitor",
              "60s wake",
              "scan index",
              "real deletes",
              "oplog",
              "secondaries"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What `$text` Refuses"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cannot sit inside `$or` beside non-text clauses.",
            "Cannot be negated with `$not`; one `$text` per query.",
            "No prefix or substring match: `mongo` misses `mongodb`.",
            "Compound text puts equality fields first, and the query must supply them."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Reach For Atlas Search",
          "text": "Substring, fuzzy matching and real relevance tuning need Lucene-backed Atlas Search. `$text` is the blunt self-hosted tool."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Defaults",
          "items": [
            "Case and diacritic insensitive by default (version 3 indexes).",
            "Toggle per query with `caseSensitive` / `diacriticSensitive`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Expiry As An Index",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.sessions.createIndex({ createdAt: 1 },",
            "  { expireAfterSeconds: 3600 })",
            "// deleted 3600s after the createdAt value"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The field must be a BSON Date, or an array of Dates.",
            "Any other type and the document simply never expires.",
            "With an array of Dates the EARLIEST date decides expiry.",
            "Still a normal single-field index: serves queries and sorts."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Deletion latency is up to ~60s plus the delete itself.",
            "TTL deletes are real deletes: change streams fire, oplog grows.",
            "A mass expiry is a write storm — spread your timestamps.",
            "Runs on the primary only; secondaries apply replicated deletes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Absolute Expiry",
          "text": "Set `expireAfterSeconds: 0` and store the exact desired deletion timestamp in the field. The monitor then honours your date verbatim."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Changing The Window"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.runCommand({collMod:'sessions',",
            "  index:{keyPattern:{createdAt:1},",
            "         expireAfterSeconds: 7200}})"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Your query is either seeking or scanning, and `explain` is the only thing that will tell you which. This field guide walks the whole index zoo — B-tree mechanics, the prefix rule, ESR field order, multikey array traps, TTL, text, and the planner mistakes that quietly turn a seek back into a COLLSCAN. Ten pages of dense notes: save it before your next slow-query hunt.",
